# Which model listens to voice messages

Bee Box's agent can't hear. When a question about a voice message needs more
than the transcript ("did that sound annoyed?", "how did I pronounce
*mañana*?"), the box sends the recording to a model that accepts audio and
asks it. Choosing that model is a recurring decision, so the repository keeps
a small benchmark for it.

## How it is tested

One speaker recorded 29 short clips. Each clip tests one thing. Related clips
go to a model together, labeled only "Recording A", "Recording B", and so on,
with the same questions every time: a literal transcript, the language, the
emotion and intent, pronunciation, timing and emphasis, and any non-speech
sounds. The model is never told what the clips were meant to show. A separate
text-only model scores the answers against fixed pass/fail checks without
knowing which model wrote them. The recordings are not published.

The first run, in August 2026, compared GPT Audio 1.5, Gemini 2.5 Flash,
Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview, and Gemini 3.7 Flash, with OpenAI's Whisper as a
transcription-only baseline. The second, in October, added Gemini 3.8 Flash,
Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite, and Google's transcription-only Gemini 3.5 Transcribe,
and re-ran 3.1 Pro.

## What the models heard

### The same words, a different meaning

"That's fine, really," said once warmly and once with reluctant resignation.
Gemini 3.1 Pro described the second take as "weary, resigned, or perhaps
slightly passive-aggressive, suggesting that things are not actually fine but
they are giving up the argument." GPT Audio 1.5 called it "hesitant but still
accepting". Gemini 2.5 Flash called both takes neutral acceptance.

A second pair, "I'll see you tomorrow" said happily and then sadly, showed the
limits of the recordings as much as of the models. The Gemini models heard the
sad take as weary or resigned; GPT Audio 1.5 called it "neutral to hesitant".
No model in either run heard the happy take as happy.

### One wrong sound

The same Spanish sentence twice, the second time with *mañana* deliberately
said as *manana*.

- **Gemini 3.1 Pro and 3.7 Flash** named it exactly: the palatal nasal [ɲ] in
  the first take, an alveolar [n] in the second.
- **GPT Audio 1.5** wrote *mañana* both times and said there were "no
  noticeable differences in content or delivery."
- **Whisper** can't be asked about pronunciation, but it spelled the second
  take *manana*, which gave the change away.
- **Gemini 3.8 Flash** caught it in three of four tries. In the fourth it
  described only the pauses.
- **Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite** called both "standard Spanish pronunciation", and
  claimed the first take was whispered. It wasn't.

### Which word was stressed

"I didn't say she stole the money," five times, stressing a different word each
time. Gemini 3.1 Pro, 3.7 Flash, and 3.8 Flash found all five. GPT Audio 1.5
and Gemini 2.5 Flash each found three. Whisper wrote the same sentence five
times and, once, capitalized STOLE.

### Coughs and laughs

Two coughs, then two laughs, with no words. Whisper transcribed the coughs as
"Thank you for watching." Gemini 2.5 Flash heard a throat clear and a slow
"aah". Every other model told the coughs from the laughs, and Gemini 3.5
Transcribe returned nothing for either, which is the right answer for a
transcriber.

### A pause that means something

"Sure … I guess," with a hesitation measured at about three seconds. Gemini
3.1 Pro estimated it at "approximately three seconds" in August. Whisper and
Gemini 3.5 Transcribe wrote "Sure. I guess." and "Sure, I guess.", which keeps
the words and loses the point.

### A rolled *rr*

*El perro corre rápido por el parque*, once with a trilled *rr* and once
with a single tap. Only Gemini 3.1 Pro heard the difference, in both runs. In
October, Gemini 3.8 Flash heard "strongly articulated alveolar trills" in both
takes and "a very deep, theatrical, and gravelly voice". Both claims are wrong.

### A transcript with stage directions

A sigh, "Okay", a pause, "I was going to say Tuesday — no, Wednesday", a small
laugh, a whispered "Don't tell Maya yet", normal voice again, and a cough.
Asked for a transcript with stage directions, Gemini 3.1 Pro in October
produced:

> [Heavy sigh] Okay. [Pause 1.5s] I was gonna say Tuesday, [Pause 0.5s] no,
> Wednesday. [Laugh] [Sigh] [whispering] Don't tell Maya yet. [Pause 1s]
> [normal voice] I'll call her after lunch. [Pause 0.5s] [Cough]

That has every designed event. Whisper produced only the words.

### Plain transcription

For clean words on the page, the transcription-only models are competitive:
Whisper and GPT Audio 1.5 had the lowest error rates in August, and Gemini 3.5
Transcribe matched Whisper in October. They have their own slips. Gemini 3.5
Transcribe wrote "The rural juror rarely worries about the brewery" as "The
world-weary rarely worries about the brewery."

## Invented messages

Three generated clips contain no voice: a short electronic beep, three seconds
of silence, and three seconds of low hiss. Each went to the model alone, inside
the prompt Bee Box really sends, with the question "What exactly did the user
say in this message, and how did they sound?"

Gemini 3.8 Flash quoted a message every time, 18 of 18, and described the
speaker's tone. None of the answers hedged. A few of them:

- Beep: "Send that email."
- Beep: "Uh, hey, can you just delete that last voice message?"
- Silence: "It's raining a lot outside, so remember to bring your umbrella."
  ("The speaker (a male voice) sounded calm, clear, and helpful.")
- Hiss: "Keep your voice down." ("She speaks in a hushed, urgent whisper.")

Gemini 3.1 Pro quoted nothing. It said the clip had no speech, or, less
helpfully, that no audio was attached.

Bee Box's agent can't hear, so it takes the audio model's answer as what the
user said. An accidental send or a pocket recording looks like these clips.
The agent would get a confident quote of a request nobody made. How often
that happens with real recordings isn't measured yet.

## Rankings

**August 2026:** Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview first, Gemini 3.7 Flash a close second,
then GPT Audio 1.5 (good transcription, weak on pronunciation and prosody),
then Gemini 2.5 Flash. Bee Box moved its audio questions to 3.7 Flash.

**October 2026:** Gemini 3.1 Pro Preview first again, on every capability.
Gemini 3.8 Flash second. Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite third: fast and very cheap, but
it misses most pronunciation changes.

One more finding: Gemini 3.7 Flash, the model Bee Box asks for, is no longer
what answers. Every request for it was answered by Gemini 3.8 Flash, according
to the model version in each response. Google's model list and changelog don't
mention the substitution.

The October report recommends moving audio questions to Gemini 3.1 Pro, at
about two cents and seven seconds per question.

## Re-running it

The benchmark, its pass/fail checks, every model answer, and the full reports
(including a test-by-test account of each run) are in the repository at
[research/audio-understanding-bakeoff](https://github.com/ianb/beebox/tree/main/research/audio-understanding-bakeoff).
When a new model ships, the update is mechanical: add the model, run it next
to the current one, and write a new dated report.
